---
import PathwayLandingLayout from '../../layouts/PathwayLandingLayout.astro';
import ProcessFaqAccordion from '@/components/ProcessFaqAccordion';
import { SITE_CONFIG } from '@/lib/seo/config';

const title = 'How Indian Families Can Fund an EB-5 Investment | StudentEB5';
const metaDescription =
  'A guide for Indian families considering funding an EB-5 investment — covering gifts, source of funds, LRS/RBI planning factors, and documentation before moving capital.';
const canonicalUrl = `${SITE_CONFIG.url}/pathways/eb5-for-indian-families`;
const h1 = 'How Indian Families Can Fund an EB-5 Investment';
const subhead =
  'What families need to document before moving capital toward an EB-5 investment — and why the paper trail matters as much as the funds.';

const relatedResearch = [
  {
    title: 'EB-5 for Indian International Students',
    url: '/pathways/eb5-for-indian-students',
    description: 'Student-side decision framing for Indian F-1 applicants evaluating EB-5.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Ways to Fund EB-5 — Gifted, Loaned, and Crypto Paths',
    url: '/research/ways-to-fund-eb5-investment-2026',
    description: 'Gift, loan, and other lawful funding paths USCIS reviews for EB-5 capital.',
  },
  {
    title: 'EB-5 Pre-Investment Checklist',
    url: '/research/investment/eb5-pre-investment-checklist-part-1-f1-h1b',
    description: 'What to verify before committing capital and filing I-526E.',
  },
];

const faqItems = [
  {
    question: 'Does the student have to prove they earned the investment money themselves?',
    answer:
      'No. Gifted funds are commonly used in EB-5 cases. What matters is that the original source of the funds — usually the parent\'s — is lawful and well documented.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How far back does source of funds documentation need to go?',
    answer:
      'This depends on the specific funding history and case, and current USCIS practice can change. An immigration attorney experienced in EB-5 source-of-funds cases should review the specific facts before a family finalizes a funding plan.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What if funds come from a business the parent owns in India?',
    answer:
      'Business income can be used as a source of funds, but it typically requires more extensive documentation — business registrations, tax filings, financial statements, and a clear link between the business income and the gifted funds. This is an area where working with an adviser familiar with both Indian business documentation and USCIS expectations matters most.',
  },
];
---

<PathwayLandingLayout
  title={title}
  metaDescription={metaDescription}
  canonicalUrl={canonicalUrl}
  h1={h1}
  subhead={subhead}
  relatedResearch={relatedResearch}
  secondaryCtaText="Talk to Someone Who Understands Cross-Border EB-5 Funding"
  breadcrumbTitle="EB-5 for Indian Families"
  keywords={[
    'Indian parents EB-5 funding',
    'EB-5 gift funds India',
    'LRS EB-5 remittance',
    'fund EB-5 for child in US',
  ]}
>
  <Fragment slot="extraCompliance">
    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed border border-border rounded-lg bg-secondary/40 p-4 m-0">
      This page provides educational information only and does not constitute legal, financial, tax, or immigration
      advice. Consult a qualified immigration attorney and a cross-border tax adviser before moving investment capital
      or entering into a gift arrangement.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <h2>Who this page is for</h2>
  <p>
    In many EB-5 cases involving Indian international students or young professionals, the child is the investor of
    record, but a parent or other family member is the actual source of the funds. This is a normal and accepted
    structure in EB-5 — USCIS does not require the investor to have personally earned the investment capital. What USCIS
    does require is a clear, documented, lawful trail showing where the money came from and how it reached the investor.
    This page focuses on that funding and documentation process; for the broader student decision (EB-5 vs. the H-1B
    path), see our guide to
    <a href="/pathways/eb5-for-indian-students">EB-5 for Indian international students</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>How the gift structure typically works</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Establish the gift relationship.</strong> A parent (or other family member) formally gifts funds to the
      student investor, documented with a signed gift letter or deed of gift.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Document the parent's source of funds.</strong> USCIS looks through to the original source of the gifted
      funds, not just the gift itself. This can include income records, business ownership documentation, property sale
      records, or other lawful sources going back multiple years.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Trace the path of funds.</strong> Bank records showing the money moving from the parent's account to the
      student's account, and then to the new commercial enterprise or regional center escrow account, need to form a
      complete, unbroken paper trail.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Plan for LRS and RBI compliance.</strong> India's Liberalised Remittance Scheme (LRS) governs how
      individuals can remit funds abroad, and Reserve Bank of India (RBI) rules affect how investment capital can leave
      India. These limits and procedures change. Families should confirm current requirements with a cross-border
      tax/financial adviser familiar with RBI regulations rather than relying on outdated figures — see official
      <a href="https://www.rbi.org.in/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">RBI</a> materials for authoritative
      updates.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Prepare documentation before, not after.</strong> Gathering these records early — ideally before a project
      is selected or a petition is filed — gives families more time to address any gaps.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Common documentation questions</h2>
  <div class="not-prose mb-8">
    <ProcessFaqAccordion client:load items={faqItems} />
  </div>

  <h2>Tax and advisory considerations</h2>
  <p>
    Moving investment capital across borders can carry tax implications on both sides — U.S. and India — separate from
    the immigration process itself. This page does not provide tax or legal advice. Families considering this path
    typically work with a cross-border tax adviser and a U.S. immigration attorney together, early in the process,
    rather than addressing tax questions after funds have already moved. For funding-path options and a pre-filing
    checklist, see
    <a href="/research/ways-to-fund-eb5-investment-2026">ways to fund an EB-5 investment</a> and the
    <a href="/research/investment/eb5-pre-investment-checklist-part-1-f1-h1b">EB-5 pre-investment checklist</a>.
  </p>
</PathwayLandingLayout>
